      "itemSourceType": null,
      "likeCount": 0,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5b40695ef51b8b546f156d7c",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Yeah, an avatar \"Benchmark\" so to speak. It really SHOULD be done server-side to prevent tampering, and have a controlled environment, like you said. It would also allow for a possible \"performance rating\" stat, which could be used in the future to, perhaps, have a certain local setting to only display avatars that fit into \"high/med/low\" performance for weaker PCs\n\nNot to mention, I'm sure there are plenty of creators out there that would appreciate the far more detailed performance reports that their avatars could then provide. For example, it could help pinpoint specific emotes, materials, animations, etc. that are causing lag.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2018-07-07T07:18:54.183Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Avatars should not have poly count caps, or materials count caps. Instead I propose a system that measures the actual performance of an avatar. Before a users uploads a file, it should go through each of the avatar's emotes/gestures and measure the average FPS of each one, along with a standard catch all emote, like a back flip. If one of the emotes uses too much resources, it shouldn't upload, and it should show the user the offending emote/gesture. Perhaps this verification/calculation could be done on the VRChat server side, which conditions are kept constant. Alternatively, it could be done on hosts' computers, but in order to adjust for variable setups, there would need to be a standard test scene, which FPS and other metrics could be compared to. (Performance ratings of an avatar should be more or less the same regardless of what computer a user is using)",
  "error": null,
